Subject: [PATCH] fix(gateway): a Codex subagent keeps its own account's cache
 (#5698)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every request of a Codex chat names the chat's own session_id, its
subagents' too, and the gateway kept one affinity stick for the session.
Six Codex multi-agent V1 subagents over three accounts took their first
tasks on three accounts, and the second round read that one stick
(kept=true, why=session) — all six went to the account that had answered
last, and what each vendor had cached of a subagent's thread was left
behind (Foreverrainy: WorkBuddy's three accounts, twelve requests, the
second round all on one).

stickKey now keeps a Codex subagent's stick apart, by the thread id its
turn metadata names (codexSubagentThread), when that thread is not the
session's own, on a key of its own ending "|thread:" — not Claude Code's
"|agent:", which withLead would keep with the session's account under
Smart; a Codex subagent's first turn still spreads over the accounts as
a session of its own, and its later turns stay with whoever answered it,
whose cache holds its thread. The lead's key is the session's as before,
so its stick, and leadFirst's read of it by the thread id, are as they
were; a chat whose metadata names no other thread keeps the one stick it
had. The turn metadata is read only behind the fromCodex header gate, so
no other agent's request parses it.

Run (darwin/arm64, at this commit):
- go test -run 'TestCodexSubagentsKeepTheirOwnAccounts|
  TestCodexChatWithoutSubagentsKeepsItsAffinity' ./internal/gateway: red
  at the parent aa3d11d5b (the three workers all on one account, kept
  why=session; their sticks one shared entry), green here, over In turn
  and Smart alike; the chat without subagents green at the parent and
  here (its stick still the session's alone).
- go test -count=1 ./internal/gateway: ok; -race over the affinity and
  Codex families: ok. gofmt -l, GOOS=linux/windows go vet: clean.

